# Operator panel only. Read by `npm run admin`, and by nothing else.
#
# This file holds the credential that can sign, transfer and launch tokens.
# It is loaded AFTER .env, and no variable may appear in both: the public
# units read .env alone, and rh-oracle-api refuses to start if BANKR_API_KEY
# reaches it.
#
#   npm run secrets -- --admin        # fills ADMIN_AUTH_SECRET
#   sudo chmod 600 /opt/rh-oracle/.env.admin

# The wallet / agent / token-launch key. ADMIN BOX ONLY.
# bankr.bot/api-keys: Wallet API on, Read Only off, IP allowlist set to this
# box. Leave Agent API off unless something actually prompts the agent.
# Verify what it can really do with: npm run bankr:scope
BANKR_API_KEY=

# Signs operator-panel sessions. Deliberately NOT the same value as
# AUTH_SECRET: a session minted by the public site must not be a valid admin
# session. Generated by `npm run secrets -- --admin`.
ADMIN_AUTH_SECRET=

# Addresses allowed to open the panel, comma separated. Empty means the panel
# refuses to start -- there is no "anyone who signs in" mode. Use the address
# you will actually sign with in the browser, not the agent's own wallet.
ADMIN_ADDRESSES=

# Shown in the message you sign, so it is obvious which panel you are
# authorising. Any stable name will do; it is not resolved.
# ADMIN_DOMAIN=oracle-admin.local

# Loopback by default. Reach it with:  ssh -L 8090:127.0.0.1:8090 <box>
# ADMIN_HOST=127.0.0.1
# ADMIN_PORT=8090

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- publishing
#
# Setting these two puts the panel behind a hostname instead of an ssh tunnel.
# They change the panel's BEHAVIOUR, not just its address, because the panel
# had written down three decisions as safe on the grounds that it was
# unreachable. In this mode it:
#
#   - forces Secure on the session cookie, whatever ADMIN_SECURE_COOKIE says;
#   - shortens the session from 12h to 2h (override with ADMIN_SESSION_HOURS);
#   - stops telling a failed sign-in whether the address is an owner — the
#     reason still goes to the journal, where only you read it;
#   - rate limits /admin/nonce and /admin/verify per client address;
#   - drops the deployment details from the unauthenticated /admin/health;
#   - requires ADMIN_AUTH_SECRET to be at least 32 characters, not 16;
#   - sends HSTS.
#
# Set ADMIN_HOST to an address only the reverse proxy can reach. On this box
# Caddy runs in Docker, so that is the gateway of its network — the same
# address the API uses — and never 0.0.0.0.
#
#   docker inspect <caddy-container> \
#     --format '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.Gateway}}{{end}}'
#
# Then add ops/rh-oracle-admin-docker-caddy.snippet to the Caddyfile.
#
# Binding outward WITHOUT ADMIN_ALLOW_REMOTE=1 is a refusal to start, not a
# warning: the flag is what turns all of the above on, so a panel bound
# outward without it would be the unhardened one on a public address.
#
# ADMIN_HOST=172.18.0.1
# ADMIN_ALLOW_REMOTE=1

# Session lifetime in hours. Defaults to 12 on loopback and 2 when published.
# ADMIN_SESSION_HOURS=2

# Forced on when the panel is published, so it only matters on loopback — and
# there it should stay unset: over an ssh tunnel the panel is plain http on
# localhost, where a Secure cookie is never sent and the login merely appears
# to fail. Setting it to 0 while published is refused at boot.
# ADMIN_SECURE_COOKIE=1
